# /clean — post-merge tidy

One-shot post-merge cleanup. The git work happens in `bin/smriti-clean` (non-interactive); the skill body's only AI job is to issue the decision-brief AskUserQuestion before any branch is deleted.

## Step 1: Plan

Run the script's `--plan` mode. It inspects the current git state, queries GitHub for merged PRs (falls back to `git branch --merged` when `gh` is unavailable), and returns one of four actions: `single`, `batch`, `noop`, `refuse`. Capture the JSON.

```bash
PLAN=$(smriti clean --plan 2>/dev/null) || { echo "/clean: smriti clean --plan failed"; exit 0; }
ACTION=$(printf '%s' "$PLAN" | jq -r .action)
DETECTION=$(printf '%s' "$PLAN" | jq -r .detection)
REFUSAL_REASON=$(printf '%s' "$PLAN" | jq -r '.refusal_reason // empty')
CURRENT_BRANCH=$(printf '%s' "$PLAN" | jq -r .current_branch)
```

**If `DETECTION = "git-fallback"`**, prefix any subsequent output with this one-line note:

> `(detection degraded: gh unavailable; using git-only — squash-merged branches may not appear)`

`gh` is the only way to reliably detect merged branches in a squash-merge workflow. If you're seeing `git-fallback`, install/auth `gh` for accurate detection — otherwise `/clean` may report nothing to clean when there actually are merged branches.

## Step 2: Dispatch on `ACTION`

### `ACTION = "refuse"`

The script already decided this won't work. Print the refusal reason verbatim and exit — no AskUserQuestion (fake interactivity).

```bash
echo "/clean refuses: $REFUSAL_REASON"
exit 0
```

Examples of refusal: dirty working tree, detached HEAD, unborn repo. The user fixes the precondition and re-runs.

### `ACTION = "noop"`

Nothing to do — either you're on the default branch with no merged candidates, or you're on a feature branch that isn't recognized as merged. Print the reason and exit.

```bash
echo "/clean: $REFUSAL_REASON"
exit 0
```

### `ACTION = "single"`

You're on a feature branch that's been merged. Issue a decision-brief AskUserQuestion before deletion. The question shape (decision-brief, see top of skill):

```
D1 — Delete merged branch and switch to <default>?

ELI10: <current branch> was merged (PR #<merged_pr or "detected via git">).
       Clean-up moves you to <default>, pulls latest, deletes the local
       branch, and prunes stale remote refs.

Stakes if we pick wrong: deleting before the squash actually lands on
       <default> throws away the only local copy of your changes — though
       gh's merged-state check makes that very unlikely.

Recommendation: Delete + switch — gh confirms PR #<n> merged.

(recommended) Delete + switch to <default>
  ✅ Standard post-merge tidy — checkout, pull, delete, prune in one step
  ✅ Surfaces a no-op when the script's revalidation disagrees with --plan
  ❌ Local branch is gone; any uncommitted history goes with it

Switch only (keep branch)
  ✅ Conservative — keeps a local copy in case something downstream surprises
  ✅ Useful when you want to compare or cherry-pick before deleting
  ❌ Branch lingers; next /clean run will offer it again

Abort
  ✅ No cons — escape hatch
```

Issue the AskUserQuestion. On `Delete + switch`, run `smriti clean --branch "$CURRENT_BRANCH"` and surface its output. On `Switch only`, run `git checkout <default> && git pull --ff-only` (don't call the script; skip the deletion). On `Abort`, exit cleanly.

If `smriti clean --branch` exits non-zero, surface stderr verbatim. Common non-zero outcomes after a `single` plan:

| Code | Meaning | What to surface |
|---|---|---|
| 4 | revalidation disagrees — script no longer thinks it's merged | "branch state changed between plan and execute; re-run /clean" |
| 5 | branch-local soft refusal (unpushed commits, no upstream) | the stderr message lists the unpushed commits |
| 8 | state changed (branch deleted or renamed under us) | "branch is gone; re-run /clean" |

For exit 5, offer a follow-up AskUserQuestion: "retry with `--force` (deletes despite unpushed work)?" Decision-brief shape; **do** ask, because `--force` is a deliberate user choice.

### `ACTION = "batch"`

You're on the default branch with one or more merged candidates. Decision-brief with the **explicit candidate list inline** — no blind approval.

```
D1 — Delete <N> merged branches?

ELI10: <N> local branches were merged into <default>. Cleanup deletes
       each, then prunes stale remote refs.

Candidates:
  • <branch-1>  (PR #<n> | merged via git-fallback)
  • <branch-2>  (PR #<n> | merged via git-fallback)
  • ...

Stakes if we pick wrong: any branch that's actually still in use disappears
       locally; safe candidates have safe_to_delete=true in --plan output.
       Branches flagged unsafe will be skipped-with-note unless --force.

Recommendation: Delete all — every candidate has a merged-state signal.

(recommended) Delete all
  ✅ One-shot cleanup; matches the standard post-merge tidy
  ✅ Skips branches with unpushed work (per safety matrix) unless --force
  ❌ N branches gone in one keystroke; the JSON candidate list is your one
     chance to spot a false positive

Pick branches one at a time
  ✅ Maximum control — review each candidate individually
  ✅ Right when you've been doing exploratory branch work
  ❌ Slower; one AskUserQuestion per candidate

Abort
  ✅ No cons — escape hatch
```

On `Delete all`, run `smriti clean --all` and surface its output (the script prints `(skipped: X — <reason>)` for branch-local soft refusals and a final `N deleted, K skipped` summary). On `Pick one at a time`, iterate over `candidates[]` and re-enter the single-branch flow for each. On `Abort`, exit cleanly.

If `smriti clean --all` exits 2 (dirty) or 6 (detached/unborn), surface stderr — these are global preconditions that hard-stop the batch.

## Step 3: Handoff

After a successful run print one line of next-step guidance:

- **Single deleted, on default branch** → *"Branch cleaned. Ready for the next ticket."*
- **Batch deleted** → *"N branch(es) cleaned. Ready for the next ticket."*
- **Noop / refuse** → no handoff line (the reason was already surfaced).

Don't auto-invoke anything; the user drives.

